# CODEOWNERS — who is asked to review, and, since 7 September 2026, whose
# approval counts.
#
# Two groups, per GOVERNANCE.md §3, CORE-TEAM.md and .github/REVIEW_AND_MERGE.md §4:
#
#   core team    (CORE-TEAM.md)    writes the framework and holds write
#                                  authority over the specification. §4: it
#                                  owns spec/, rfcs/, public-review/,
#                                  evidence/ "and everything not claimed by a
#                                  more specific rule" -- which is why the
#                                  default below is the core team and not the
#                                  maintainers.
#   maintainers  (MAINTAINERS.md)  core team members carrying the operational
#                                  duties. They own what the project
#                                  *promises* -- governance, licences,
#                                  citation, the role files -- and the
#                                  guardrails themselves. Whoever can change
#                                  the guardrails can remove them.
#
# Ownership and review tier are different questions. evidence/ and
# public-review/ are core-team-owned and still STANDARD tier: being owned means
# someone specific is asked to look; being elevated means more than one must
# approve. The tier lives in .github/workflows/review-tier.yml, not here.
#
# Individual handles, not team slugs. REVIEW_AND_MERGE.md §4 warns that GitHub
# silently requests nobody for a team that does not exist, and review-tier.yml
# refuses to guess at a team's membership rather than counting it as zero or as
# two. Individuals are the honest form until the teams exist in the org.
#
# review-tier.yml reads this file FROM THE BASE COMMIT of a pull request, so a
# change here cannot make its own pull request self-approvable.

# --- default: the core team, per REVIEW_AND_MERGE.md §4 -------------------
